/* penalty.lps — Kowalski, ch. 1 §1.5: the penalty sentence as an inhibitor.
 *
 * The fourth sentence of the Emergency Notice, on its own, because the book
 * makes a point of it: "There is a 50 pound penalty for improper use" is not a
 * description of a fine schedule. It is there to *stop you pressing the
 * button*, and the logical form that does that is a constraint.
 *
 * The difference is observable rather than philosophical. As a constraint the
 * improper press never happens; as a belief it happens and then a fine
 * follows. Run this and underground.lps and compare the traces.
 */

maxTime(6).

fluents  emergency(_), fined(_, _).
events   emergency_occurs(_), press(_).
actions  fine(_, _), warn(_).

emergency_occurs(K) initiates emergency(K).
fine(Who, Amount)   initiates fined(Who, Amount).

%  As a *belief*: improper use is followed by a fine.
if   press(Who) from _ to T, not emergency(_) at T
then fine(Who, 50) from T to T2.

%  As a *constraint*, the same sentence forbids the state instead. An agent
%  that has this cannot press improperly at all — which is what a notice is
%  for. (Both are here so the two readings can be run side by side; a real
%  program would choose.)
false fined(Who, _), not offender(Who).

offender(vandal).

observe press(vandal) from 2 to 3.
